ssed)\n14. was (Stressed)\n15. the (Usually unstressed, but can be stressed)\n16. success (Stressed)\n17. of (Usually unstressed, but can be stressed)\n18. uh (Vocalization, often treated as a word/pause filler, carries vocal emphasis)\n19. of (Usually unstressed, but can be stressed)\n20. being (Stressed)\n21. on (Stressed)\n22. the (Usually unstressed, but can be stressed)\n23. Obama (Stressed)\n24. campaign (Stressed)\n25. and (Can be unstressed, but often stressed for emphasis or clarity)\n26. seeing (Stressed)\n27. their (Stressed)\n28. their (Stressed)\n29. ground (Stressed)\n30. operation (Stressed)\n31. um (Vocalization, often treated as a word/pause filler, carries vocal emphasis)\n32. it (Stressed)\n33. really (Stressed)\n34. takes (Stressed)\n35. a (Usually unstressed, but can be stressed)\n36. door-to-door (Stressed)\n37. approach (Stressed)\n38. and (Can be unstressed, but often stressed for emphasis or clarity)\n39. I (Stressed)\n40. think (Stressed)\n41. that's (Stressed)\n42. something (Stressed)\n\n**Step 3: Count the total number of words.**\nCounting the tokens identified above: There are 42 tokens/words.\n\n**Step 4: Re-evaluating the definition based on typical QA constraints.**\nIn linguistic analysis, function words like "and," "the," "of," "a" are often considered unstressed unless they carry contrastive stress. If we strictly count only words that *must* carry stress (content words + pronouns/auxiliaries that are often stressed):\n\nContent words/Pronouns/Stronger function words:\n1. is (Stressed)\n2. interesting (Stressed)\n3. effective (Stressed)\n4. because (Stressed)\n5. they're (Stressed)\n6. very (Stressed)\n7. grassroots (Stressed)\n8. oriented (Stressed)
